    webapp   -> ao-webodm-webapp   (WO_HOST)
    worker   -> ao-webodm-worker
    db       -> ao-webodm-db       (wait-for-postgres.sh, WO_DATABASE_HOST)
    broker   -> ao-webodm-broker   (WO_BROKER, wait-for-it)
    nodeodm  -> ao-nodeodm
    sales-db -> ao-sales-db        (bootstrap-sales-db.sh)

  LATENT BUG UNCOVERED. The WebODM app had never been told where its database
  is: /webodm/webodm/settings.py defaults WO_DATABASE_HOST to the literal "db".
  That happened to work only because the container was literally named db. The
  rename broke it - the app logged "could not translate host name db" - which
  exposed the real defect. Fixed by setting WO_DATABASE_HOST=ao-webodm-db
  explicitly in the web and worker definitions, so the host is stated rather
  than inherited from a coincidental name. A first sed pass also double-applied
  the broker prefix in the worker; caught and corrected before deploy.

  A repo-side grep had missed the host references because they sit inside the
  Exec= startup script, not in Environment/Volume lines. Read the container logs
  instead of trusting the grep.

  Verified: all 5 mapping units + ao-sales-db active; 0 "could not translate"
  errors after restart; webapp answers HTTP 302 (normal redirect, was a 500
  while the DB was unreachable); worker reaches webapp:8000 and broker:6379;
  ao-sales-db healthy with 23 tables in salesdb. WebODM db itself is 0 tables,
  which is the pre-existing state and is untouched.

