2026-09-30 — duplicate databases repaired: webodm migrated, both dropped
  Backups first: backups/duplicate-db-20260930/{webodm,mastodon}.{dump,sql},
  pg_restore-verified (85 and 198 table entries), and the plain SQL confirmed to
  contain the real rows in COPY form. backups/ is gitignored, so this stays local.

  KEY DISCOVERY - the app was reading the wrong database. The container has both
  `webodm` and `webodm_dev`; pg_stat_activity showed the app connected to
  webodm_dev. My first restore attempt targeted `webodm` and would have restored
  into a database nothing reads. Also the container runs PostgreSQL 9.5
  (webodm/webodm_db image) against a host cluster of 18, so pg_restore refused the
  18.x archive ("unsupported version 1.16"). Migrated data-only instead.

  MIGRATION webodm -> webodm_dev
    - 28 tables shared, 4 container-only, none missing: schema compatible.
    - WebODM's own config tables (app_basemap, app_preset, app_plugin, app_theme)
      were NOT migrated: the running instance owns them and importing the older
      copies risked collision. Only app_basemap failed on a NOT NULL column
      anyway (older schema allowed NULLs the current one does not).
    - Loaded 159 rows across 9 tables in FK-safe order (auth_* first, then
      app_profile, nodeodm_processingnode, then app_project/app_task). First
      attempt hit an FK error on app_project.owner_id and a second on
      app_task.processing_node_id; both fixed by ordering, not by dropping data.
    - Result verified: 10 projects, 10 tasks, all task->project links intact,
      2 users. App returns HTTP 302 with zero database errors.

  DROPPED from the host cluster (both had zero connections; both guarded):
    - mastodon  (14 MB, orphan from the alwayson-sales era; contradicted
      instance-policy.yaml, which says Mastodon does not reuse host PG18)
    - webodm    (18 MB; its data now lives in the container)
  Host cluster is now cordadb, grafana, metabase, modeldb, postgres, reporting,
  salesdb - no duplicates.

  Post-change verification: all 14 services active; Mastodon still v4.3.7 on
  mastodon.300x3.com with @aoadmin (Owner) and @bot approved and 1 remote
  account; ao-sales-db 23 tables; a_fab 1 table; webodm_dev 32 tables.

  A pkexec guard aborted the webodm drop because podman is not on the root PATH.
  The abort was correct behaviour - the check could not run. Verified the
  container copy from the user session first, then dropped with the guard.
