  different values (ALWAYSON 48 chars, ao-mastodon 40 chars; hashes differ).
  fetch-mastodon-env.sh and the bridge read ao-mastodon and write
  ao-secrets/mastodon.env, which is what the running web/sidekiq/streaming
  containers authenticate with. fetch-kwallet-secret.sh reads ALWAYSON and
  writes ao-secrets/mastodon-db.env for the database container's
  POSTGRES_PASSWORD. PostgreSQL only uses POSTGRES_PASSWORD when the data
  directory is empty, so in steady state the ALWAYSON value is inert. Verified
  the ALWAYSON value does authenticate against the HOST cluster as role
  mastodon, which is a different server from the mastodon-db container. A fresh
  mastodon-dbdata would therefore create the role with one value while the app
  connects with the other - an immediate failure. Documented as a hazard and
  raised as open item 14.1.2(1).

  NAMING EXCEPTION - the ALWAYSON wallet folder is the one infrastructure name
  that does not carry the ao- prefix. fetch-kwallet-secret.sh hardcodes it, so
  renaming is a coordinated change. Raised as open item 14.1.2(2) rather than
  done unilaterally, since it touches live secret lookup.

  Also noted: env files are written to two roots - ao-secrets/ for Mastodon and
  reporting, ~/secrets/ for sales, webodm and fabrication databases. Both are
  gitignored so there is no exposure, but it should converge.

