2026-09-30 — /dev/heltec-v3 was pointing at the DRONE-RADIO (live mislabel)
  Operator noted the Heltec ambiguity had already presented itself, which is why
  the radios carry distinct names. Confirmed and found the concrete damage.

  config/field/heltec-v3/udev/99-ao-heltec.rules matched on vendor/product
  alone (10c4:ea60). Both LoRa radios are CP2102 boards reporting those exact
  ids AND the same placeholder serial "..._0001", so the rule fired on them.
  Result on this host:
      /dev/heltec-v3 -> ttyUSB0
  and ttyUSB0 is the DRONE-RADIO (by-path pci-0000:05:00.0-usb-0:1:1.0-port0).
  Any consumer using that symlink would have opened the drone radio's private
  Reticulum RNode and written to it. PEOPLE-RADIO is ttyUSB1 and was not claimed
  because the symlink name is singular.

  FIX
    - rule now requires a real serial: ENV{ID_SERIAL_SHORT}!="0001" excludes the
      generic clones, so neither radio can be claimed
    - removed the stale /dev/heltec-v3 symlink
    - reload + tty trigger; /dev/heltec-v3 is now correctly absent
    - the installed rule was backed up first
  Reticulum itself never used the symlink - it addresses both radios by
  /dev/serial/by-path topology, which is unambiguous. So no mesh traffic was at
  risk; the risk was to any future ao-field consumer.

  Still to confirm when the Heltec is connected: its real by-path and serial. If
  it also reports 0001, the rule must be re-keyed on by-path instead of serial.

