2026-10-01  ao-build-update scaffold created and deployed (README 5.2.1)
================================================================================

SCOPE
  Scaffolded the ao-build-update controlled software-update acquisition adapter.
  Deployed but deliberately NOT enabled. No workload was touched, no image was
  installed, no package upgraded, no service restarted.

CORRECTION TO README 5.1 GROUP B
  The group B row previously placed this adapter on "ao-admin (build/update
  duty)". ao-admin is Internal=true, which makes the adapter's only function
  impossible. Verified directly on this host:
    $ podman run --rm --network ao-admin alpine nslookup registry-1.docker.io
    ** server can't find registry-1.docker.io: NXDOMAIN
    $ podman run --rm --network ao-reporting-egress alpine nc -z registry-1.docker.io 443
    TCP OK
  A container on ao-admin cannot pull an image. The adapter was inert by
  construction. Placed instead on its own egress network ao-build-update,
  10.89.13.0/24, Internal=false, following the ao-reporting-egress precedent.
  Re-probed on the new network: DNS resolves, TCP 443 OK.

FILES ADDED
  quadlet/build-update/ao-build-update.network
  quadlet/build-update/ao-build-update.container
  config/build-update/registry-allowlist.yaml
  scripts/build-update/ao-build-update.py

FILES MODIFIED
  README.md                                       (5.2.1 added; 5.1 group B and
                                                  19.1 item 3 corrected)
  config/platform/network-cidrs.yaml             (regenerated by the checker)
  config/platform/topology-model.yaml            (planned -> scaffolded)
  scripts/validation/check-network-isolation.sh  (added to egress list)

COMMANDS
  ./scripts/deploy/deploy-quadlet-domain.sh build-update
  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  podman network create --internal=false --subnet 10.89.13.0/24 ao-build-update
  ./scripts/validation/check-network-isolation.sh   -> exit 0
  systemctl --user start ao-build-update.service    -> exit 0

VERIFICATION
  Isolation check green; ao-build-update asserted Internal=false at 10.89.13.0/24.
  Unit generates and is NOT enabled: it carries no WantedBy=.
  Host-side regression, all six cases as expected:
    allowlisted + digest-pinned        -> 0
    unpinned tag                       -> 4
    not-allowlisted host               -> 4
    denied host (packages.ros.org)     -> 4
    unqualified reference              -> 2
    ghcr.io + digest-pinned            -> 0
  Container-side: unpinned tag -> 4, denied host -> 4, default --plan -> 0.
  The container run wrote through its bind mounts to the host evidence paths
  /ALWAYSON/logs/operations/build-update-audit.log and
  /ALWAYSON/data/build-update/staging/, confirmed on the host.
  --fetch against a digest absent from the local store reports resolved=false
  and resolved_digest=null: it does not claim a resolution it did not make.

THREE DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED DURING VERIFICATION
  1. ao-build-update.network was missing its [Network] group header, so
     quadlet-generator rejected it ("key file does not start with a group") and
     no service was generated. Caught only by reading the generator log: the
     deploy script itself reported success. Header restored.
  2. The allowlist listed registry-1.docker.io, the Docker Hub API endpoint,
     while image references carry the docker.io prefix. Every legitimate
     docker.io reference was rejected as NOT-ALLOWLISTED. Corrected to
     docker.io, with the endpoint recorded as a comment, because the two are
     not the same string.
  3. The acquisition script hardcoded /ALWAYSON paths, which do not exist
     inside the container. The first real unit run exited 3 (config error). The
     script now resolves its allowlist, staging dir, and audit log through
     AO_ROOT plus three explicit environment overrides set by the unit, so a
     host run and a container run write evidence to the same files.

OPEN, FOR THE OPERATOR
  - Enabling the unit permanently, and any decision to automate acquisition. It
    reaches the public internet, so this is an operator decision.
  - The build/update scope question: the name implies builds, the specified role
    is updates only. The Gazebo Containerfiles under GAZEBO/containers/ have no
    owner, no digest capture, and no update audit. Recorded in README 5.2.1.
  - packages.ros.org stays on the adapter deny list. It fails TLS verification
    from this host (cert CN=*.osuosl.org does not cover the name) and that
    verification is deliberately not disabled.

