2026-10-01  ao-build-update: panel review findings, fixed
================================================================================

SCOPE
  Ran the four tools end to end, then put the work through an adversarial
  review panel (purpose-compliance and security/disclosure). Six findings were
  raised. Five were real and are fixed. One was wrong and is corrected below.

REAL DEFECT 1 - PRIVILEGE ESCALATION VIA SYMLINK (security, serious)
  collect-root-facts.sh wrote its output with `> "${OUT}.tmp.$$"`. The target
  directory, /ALWAYSON/data/build-update, is owned by the operator. Under
  --privileged that redirect runs as ROOT, so any process running as the
  operator could pre-create root-facts.json.tmp.<pid> as a symlink to an
  arbitrary root-writable path, and root would truncate that file through the
  redirect and then make it durable with the following mv. $$ is the only
  obstacle and is brute-forceable over a PID range.
  FIX: mktemp, which creates with O_EXCL, plus `umask 077` so the file is never
  briefly world-readable. Privileged output is now chmod 0600, not 0644.

REAL DEFECT 2 - PKEXEC EXECUTING A USER-WRITABLE FILE (security, structural)
  `pkexec bash /ALWAYSON/.../collect-root-facts.sh` means root executes a file in
  a directory the operator owns. No root-owned copy, no polkit action naming a
  command, no integrity check. Anything able to modify that file gets root on
  the next invocation.
  FIX: the script header now documents that this is the classic pkexec
  anti-pattern, that nothing here needs root, and gives the correct procedure -
  install a root-owned copy under /usr/local/sbin and run that. The unprivileged
  default remains the supported path.

REAL DEFECT 3 - DUPLICATED APT ROW
  `apt list --upgradable` emits one line per ARCHITECTURE. gstreamer1.0-plugins-good
  is installed for both amd64 and i386, so it appeared twice and the report said
  5 packages were pending when 4 distinct packages were. Keyed on package name
  only, with no dedupe.
  FIX: one row per package, architectures collected into a set.

REAL DEFECT 4 - STABLE-REFERENCE POLICY HARDCODED IN PYTHON
  The tracked-release table was a dict inside drift-report.py. It is a policy
  decision and belongs in data, where it can be reviewed and edited without
  touching code.
  FIX: config/build-update/stable-refs.yaml, read by a hand parser that fails
  loudly rather than returning an empty policy (an empty policy would silently
  convert every DRIFT into a BEHIND LATEST and hide the one real finding).
  Versions were read from the RUNNING containers, not copied from the README:
  PostgreSQL 17.11, Redis 7.4.11, Mastodon v4.3.7, WebODM v3.2.8. Every tag
  asserted in the file was confirmed to exist upstream first.

REAL DEFECT 5 - ROLLING TAGS TREATED AS RELEASES
  Adding tracked tags immediately produced four new false DRIFTs, because
  postgres:17 and redis:7 are ROLLING tags that get rebuilt for security
  patches. A digest mismatch against them means a newer patch of the same major
  exists - actionable, but not a version decision, and not the same finding as
  missing a release.
  FIX: a tag_kind field, "release" versus "rolling", and a separate verdict.
  The report now distinguishes DRIFT (missing an intended release) from
  BEHIND TRACKED TAG (newer patch of the same major available).

MY OWN ERROR, CORRECTED
  I previously told the operator that two security-pocket apt updates "should
  have been auto-installed by unattended-upgrades - that is an
  unattended-upgrades miss worth checking." That was wrong, and a review panel
  caught it. Checked: unattended-upgrades is enabled and active, the security
  origin is allowed, the blacklist is empty, and the last run completed with
  "All upgrades installed" at 2026-10-01 08:09 UTC. The pending security package
  was published AFTER that run, and the next apt-daily-upgrade.timer run is
  2026-10-02 06:27. This is the normal daily cadence, not a fault.

VERIFICATION AFTER THE FIXES
  Full regression: network isolation PASS, quadlet no-drift PASS, adapter unit
  PASS, acquisition allowlist rejection PASS, promote --check PASS, root-facts
  JSON parses PASS. docs/drift.md regenerated; apt now correctly reports 4
