WHAT WAS BUILT
  scripts/build-update/recommend.py -> docs/recommendations.md. Turns the drift
  report and the inventory into a ranked, explained recommendation: what needs a
  decision, why, what the risk is, and the command to run IF you choose to.

  It applies nothing. There is no code path in it that installs a package,
  promotes a digest, edits a Quadlet, deploys a unit, or restarts a service.
  Verified by grep across the whole reporting toolchain: the only matches for
  mutating verbs are prose and read-only paths like /var/lib/dpkg/status.

  This constraint is documented in the script header, in the generated document,
  in the runbook, and in README 5.2.1, so it cannot be lost by someone reading
  only one of them. Automatic updates are not built and the reporting tools are
  not to be extended to perform them.

A BUG WORTH RECORDING
  The first version recomputed container status from the stable-refs table
  instead of reading what the drift report had measured. It therefore marked all
  three Mastodon units for review, when the report had measured mastodon-web
  and mastodon-sidekiq as already in sync. A recommendation that invents a
  finding the measurement did not make is worse than no recommendation.
  FIX: drift-report.py now records a per-unit verdict in its sidecar, and
  recommend.py reads those verdicts and refuses to run if they are absent.

  As a side effect the "apt_drift" shape also had to be corrected: it is a list
  of records, not a mapping, and the first draft treated it as a mapping.

