The HTML lands under tmp/ which is gitignored; the markdown is the tracked
artifact.

SEVEN TRUNCATIONS DURING THE BUILD, all from oversized inserts
Every one produced a file that compiled but was silently missing functions -
flatsmangs returning None, ARCHIVE_PAGES undefined, snwaps() without a return,
and direct_and_unmanaged() without one. Each was caught by running it, not by
reading it. Worth recording as a pattern: chunked authoring of a long script is
the single most defect-prone part of this work, and every instance here was
caught only because the script was executed rather than inspected.
MSG
git -c user.name='ALWAYS ON Agent' -c user.email='agent@300x3.com' commit -q -F - <<'MSG'
feat(build-update): complete software provenance log

Records, for every piece of software on this host, who publishes it, where it
lives, what version is installed, what the publisher currently offers, and the
exact download link.

The other reports answer "is it stale". This one answers "what is it and where do
I get it" - the question that has to be settled before anything can be updated.

Five delivery mechanisms, because each needs a different kind of provenance:
  container  registry, repository, pinned digest, exact podman pull line
  snap       publisher and snapcraft store page
  flatpak    origin remote and flathub page
  apt        archive host, suite, component, packages.ubuntu.com page
  direct     vendor site, installed version, upstream version, download link

Coverage: 22 container images, 4229 apt packages, 16 snaps, 1 flatpak, 11
vendor-published items, plus a reference table of the 15 archives and stores the
system draws on with the ROS entry flagged unreachable.

The generator also renders styled HTML. No markdown renderer is installed on this
host (no glow, mdcat or pandoc) but python-markdown 3.10.2 is present, so it
emits a formatted page directly. The HTML is a build artifact under the gitignored
tmp/; the markdown is the tracked artifact. Regenerate rather than edit - the file
has no hand-written content.

Read-only throughout, like the rest of this toolchain.

================================================================================
2026-10-01  provenance log: versions, hashes, and an up-to-date column
================================================================================

OPERATOR ASK: "THE WHOLE THING AS VERSIONS, A HASH COMPARISON IS OK ALONGSIDE
WITH A COLUMN FOR UP TO DATE."

The table had been showing two hashes side by side, which tells a human nothing
about versions. Three changes:

1. VERSIONS EVERYWHERE. Docker Hub returns a digest for every tag in one API
   call, so a pinned digest reverse-looks-up to a publisher tag. It now PAGES,
   and stops as soon as the digest being placed is found - a one-shot call only
   covered the publisher's 100 most recent tags, so an older pin stayed a bare
   hash. Resolved on this host:
     PostgreSQL 17.11, Redis 7.4.11, Prometheus v3.15.0,
     node-exporter v1.12.1, Grafana 13.0.2, Metabase v0.63.19.1
   ghcr.io has no bulk equivalent, so its tags are resolved individually.

2. HASHES KEPT ALONGLASIDE. Pinned hash and released hash are their own columns,
   and the up-to-date verdict compares the HASHES, never the display strings -
   comparing "no version tag" or a rolling alias against a version proves
   nothing.

3. UP TO DATE column, reading: yes / behind / unknown / local build.

A PINNED MARKER was added at the operator's suggestion: a check for an immutable
digest, a cross for a floating tag. Exactly one cross on the host -
ao-ardupilot-sitl, deliberately, because the operator wants the most recent build.

PERFORMANCE
  Paging to 10 pages at a 20s timeout across thirteen repositories did not finish
  in five minutes, and resolving 24 ghcr tags per repository was the worst of it.
  Settled at 4 pages, a 12s timeout and 8 ghcr candidates: past every pin on this
  host, and it stops early anyway once a digest is placed.

THE PATTERN WORTH NAMING
  This is the ninth defect in this toolchain that reported AGREEMENT where there
  was none rather than failing: norm() reducing "sha256:d74e..." to "256", the
  same function stripping Debian revisions so alsa-ucm-conf read as current, the
  summary counting "NO" while the table emitted "**NO**", first-match tag
  selection reporting node-exporter as "v1" - a different major entirely.
  None of them raised an error. Every one was caught by RUNNING the tool and
  reading the output, never by reading the code. That is the whole method now:
  a green run means nothing until the numbers have been read against the system.

================================================================================
2026-10-01  provenance log: version resolution, caching, and why it hung
================================================================================

PERFORMANCE, which was the real work here. The generator stopped completing after
version resolution was added, and the cause was NOT the version lookups.

  MEASURED, per call, in isolation:
    containers()      16.3s   22 rows, real versions resolved
    snaps()            7.8s   16 rows
    _ghcr_tags()       7.1s   per ghcr repository, 8 tag resolutions
  Total should be well under a minute. It was not finishing in five.

  CAUSE: registry_digest_raw() was uncached, so the same image was re-fetched
  once per row that uses it. PostgreSQL appears in three units, Redis in two, and
  the Mastodon images in four. A 13-repository job became roughly 88 sequential
  HTTP round trips, each capable of burning the full timeout. The process showed
  zero CPU time throughout, which is the tell: it was blocked on network waits,
  not computing.
  FIX: memoised on (host, repo, tag).

  Also settled: 10 Hub pages at a 20s timeout was unusable, reduced to 4 pages
  and a 12s timeout, with an early stop the moment the digest being placed is
  found. 24 ghcr tag candidates cut to 8.

VERSION RESOLUTION NOW WORKS, verified per call rather than from the final file:
    Python         3.13.15-alpine3.24  ->  3.14
    PostgreSQL     17.11               ->  17.11
    Redis          7.4.11              ->  7.4.11
    Prometheus     released v3.15.0
    node-exporter  released v1.12.1
    Grafana        released 13.0.2
    Metabase       released v0.63.19.1
  The verdict now compares HASHES, never the display strings. Comparing "no
  version tag", or a rolling alias like `latest`, against a version string would
  have produced confident nonsense.

A METHOD NOTE, recorded because it nearly caused a false report here: my first
timing probe passed "docker.io/library/postgres" to a function that expects
"library/postgres", got zero entries back, and I nearly read that as the resolver
being broken. It was my probe, not the code. Measuring the real code path rather
than a hand-written one is what found the actual bottleneck.

## 2026-10-02 — Software status log: publisher/version gap closure + refresh tooling
Operator reported the install log was missing almost all publisher names and version data.
Measured first: of 339 rows, `Version here` empty on 268 and `Publisher` wrong on ALL of
them (it was derived from the download mirror hostname, e.g. `us.archive.ubuntu.com`).
Panel review run; three panel claims verified by measurement before acting:
  - `dpkg -S` treats args as SUBSTRING/GLOB patterns -> `dpkg -S ark` returns
    kf6-breeze-icon-theme. Replaced with dpkg's own /var/lib/dpkg/info/*.list manifests
    (exact, 0.07s, 299 of 307 .desktop entries resolve; the 8 unresolved are all CrossOver
    under /opt/cxoffice, installed outside dpkg).
  - `render()` and `main()` each built the whole collection, so every online run paid the
    network cost twice. main() now reuses what render() collected.
  - CRITICAL: desktop rows set pinned == released, and same_version(x, x) is True, so
    filling 257 versions would have marked 257 applications "up to date" with no upstream
    comparison performed. Desktop rows now report "?" and the sentinels were added to
    UNKNOWN so neither a false "yes" nor a false "NO" can escape.
Also fixed while measuring: snap publisher read f[5] (Notes) instead of f[4] (Publisher);
podman CreatedAt missed short image refs and tagged local refs.
Result: 230 items, 231 publishers filled, 231 versions filled (only the 3 local builds
have none, correctly). 13 behind — unchanged from before, so no new false findings.
Performance: 25.7s cold -> 10.1s warm -> 1.1s --offline, via a persistent cache under
data/build-update/cache (gitignored, tmp+rename) and a freshness banner in the document.
Refused: a systemd timer. This rewrites a tracked audit document; unattended regeneration
would overwrite one an operator is mid-review of.
READ-ONLY throughout: no package installed, promoted, deployed or restarted.
Artifacts: docs/software-status.md, docs/software-status.pdf (8 pages, rendered via
headless chrome), tmp/software-status.html. Backup of the previous pair in
backups/software-status/20261002T201958Z/. Log: logs/operations/20261002T201958Z-software-status-refresh.log
